  "reason": "With no completed experiments, the most informative first step is to establish the simplest, most interpretable baseline: RNA-only with PCA (2000 components) and a single profile. This configuration isolates the raw RNA signal without confounding from learned encoders, aggregation, image data, or fusion. It provides the reference performance against which we can later measure the incremental value of (a) learned RNA encoders (scvi, scgpt), (b) multiprofile aggregation (avgpool vs. transformer), (c) adding image data, and (d) fusion strategy. PCA is computationally cheap and well-understood, minimizing the risk of the first experiment being dominated by training instability or hyperparameter sensitivity. Establishing this anchor first ensures that every subsequent experiment can be interpreted as a controlled perturbation of a known quantity, maximizing the information gained per experiment under the limited budget."
}

